<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashraf Hotak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1730</span> · Also known as Ashraf Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashraf Hotak was an Afghan ruler of the Hotak dynasty who reigned as shah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> from 1725, after deposing his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-hotak">Mahmud Hotak</a>. He made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, but Nader, fighting for the Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-ii">Tahmasp II</a>, defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damghan">Damghan</a> and Murche-Khort in 1729 and drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> out of Persia. He was killed in 1730 while fleeing towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashraf-hotak.jpg" alt="Ashraf Hotak" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ashrafi coin of Ashraf Hotak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_coin_of_Ashraf_Hotak_(Ashrafi).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
